<item>
  <title>CISA adds Barracuda Email Security Gateway command injection vulnerability CVE-2026-126550 to Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog</title>
  <link>https://codedefence.in/blog/2026/11/25/cisa-adds-barracuda-esg-command-injection-cve-2026-126550-to-kev</link>
  <pubDate>Wed, 25 November 2026 18:30:00 +0300</pubDate>
  <guid isPermaLink="true">https://codedefence.in/blog/2026/11/25/cisa-adds-barracuda-esg-command-injection-cve-2026-126550-to-kev</guid>
  <cd:severity>CRITICAL</cd:severity>
  <cd:cve>CVE-2026-126550</cd:cve>
  <cd:mentions>@[Barracuda Networks](urn:li:organization:15161044)‚ @[CISA](urn:li:organization:13010360)</cd:mentions>
  <description><![CDATA[
Federal cybersecurity authorities have updated Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog directives under Binding Operational Directive 22-01, enforcing emergency remediation mandates for an actively weaponized email gateway flaw. Remote unauthenticated threat actors exploit input parsing errors during automated file extraction to obtain root execution across secure email gateway appliances.

The emergency security directive addresses Barracuda Email Security Gateway ESG appliance vulnerability CVE-2026-126550 carrying a CVSS score of 9.8. Unauthenticated remote threat actors transmit malformed email attachments containing embedded shell command characters to trigger OS command injection routines during automated attachment archive processing. Threat research confirms nation-state threat actors actively weaponize the zero-day to drop persistent web shells, monitor incoming and outgoing email transit, and harvest corporate credentials. CISA established a mandatory December 16 compliance deadline for Federal Civilian Executive Branch agencies to verify patch deployment.

Subverting edge email security gateways undermines perimeter network defenses and corporate confidentiality. Because Barracuda ESG appliances inspect external email transit, enforce domain authentication controls, and filter malicious payloads, an unauthenticated root compromise allows adversaries to intercept corporate communications, exfiltrate sensitive files, and pivot into internal subnets.

- Verify automated deployment of official Barracuda security hotfixes across all deployed Email Security Gateway appliances.
- Inspect ESG appliance file systems and mail processing logs for unverified web shell creation or unexpected outbound network connections.
- Restrict public internet access to ESG web administration portals, routing management interfaces through out-of-band VLANs.
- Invalidate active administrative user session tokens and execute mandatory credential resets across email security management accounts.

Secure email gateway defense requires patch verification and continuous file system auditing to ensure perimeter messaging infrastructure remains insulated from unauthenticated command injection exploits. #CodeDefence #Barracuda #ESG #CommandInjection #RCE #CISA #KEV #EmailSecurity #PerimeterSecurity #AppSec #PatchManagement
  ]]></description>
</item>

<item>
  <title>Active zero day exploitation targets SonicWall SonicOS SAML authentication bypass vulnerability CVE-2026-98001</title>
  <link>https://codedefence.in/blog/2026/11/25/active-zero-day-exploitation-targets-sonicwall-sonicos-saml-bypass-cve-2026-98001</link>
  <pubDate>Wed, 25 November 2026 18:30:00 +0300</pubDate>
  <guid isPermaLink="true">https://codedefence.in/blog/2026/11/25/active-zero-day-exploitation-targets-sonicwall-sonicos-saml-bypass-cve-2026-98001</guid>
  <cd:severity>CRITICAL</cd:severity>
  <cd:cve>CVE-2026-98001</cd:cve>
  <cd:mentions>@[SonicWall](urn:li:organization:15161044)‚ @[CISA](urn:li:organization:13010360)</cd:mentions>
  <description><![CDATA[
Perimeter security advisories published today warn of active wild attacks targeting firewall appliances and SSL VPN access gateways. Remote unauthenticated threat actors exploit logic flaws within single sign-on authentication handlers to bypass multi-factor authentication controls and establish unauthorized administrative sessions.

The emergency security bulletin covers SonicWall SonicOS software vulnerability CVE-2026-98001 carrying a CVSS score of 9.8 and impacting Gen 6, Gen 7, and NSv virtual firewalls. Remote threat actors send malformed SAML authentication responses containing manipulated cryptographic assertions to bypass single sign-on authentication checks. Successful exploitation grants unauthenticated adversaries administrative access to firewall management web interfaces and NetExtender SSL VPN gateways without valid user credentials. Threat telemetry confirms automated scanning botnets are actively exploiting exposed firewall management interfaces worldwide. SonicWall released emergency firmware updates to remediate active intrusion attempts.

Subverting core firewall controllers and remote access gateways compromises network microsegmentation and edge access boundaries. Because SonicWall firewalls manage site-to-site IPsec tunnels, enforce identity-based access rules, and route corporate network traffic, an unauthenticated SAML authentication bypass allows adversaries to hijack user sessions, inspect encrypted traffic, and move laterally across production subnets.

- Upgrade SonicOS firmware immediately across physical firewalls and NSv virtual appliances to emergency hotfix maintenance releases.
- Audit SAML identity provider configuration logs for anomalous single sign-on authentication assertions originating from untrusted IP ranges.
- Restrict public internet visibility of firewall web management interfaces, placing administrative portals on isolated out-of-band VLANs.
- Invalidate active NetExtender and SMA user session tokens and execute mandatory credential resets across administrative firewall accounts.

Perimeter firewall controller defense demands immediate firmware maintenance updates and rigid identity assertion auditing to ensure edge gateways remain protected against authentication bypass exploits. #CodeDefence #SonicWall #SonicOS #SAML #AuthBypass #SSLVPN #PerimeterSecurity #AppSec #PatchManagement #CISA
  ]]></description>
</item>

<item>
  <title>Critical N-able N-central agent remote command execution flaw CVE-2026-118902 targeted in widespread enterprise scans</title>
  <link>https://codedefence.in/blog/2026/11/25/critical-n-able-n-central-agent-rce-flaw-cve-2026-118902-targeted-in-scans</link>
  <pubDate>Wed, 25 November 2026 18:30:00 +0300</pubDate>
  <guid isPermaLink="true">https://codedefence.in/blog/2026/11/25/critical-n-able-n-central-agent-rce-flaw-cve-2026-118902-targeted-in-scans</guid>
  <cd:severity>CRITICAL</cd:severity>
  <cd:cve>CVE-2026-118902</cd:cve>
  <cd:mentions>@[N-able](urn:li:organization:15161044)‚ @[CISA](urn:li:organization:13010360)</cd:mentions>
  <description><![CDATA[
Remote management and monitoring RMM platform maintainers have published security advisories detailing critical vulnerabilities in enterprise endpoint management agents. Remote unauthenticated threat actors exploit deserialization flaws within agent-to-server communication channels to execute arbitrary commands under SYSTEM privileges.

The threat warning details N-able N-central agent vulnerability CVE-2026-118902 carrying a CVSS score of 9.9. Unauthenticated remote adversaries transmit crafted RPC messages to TCP port 10004 on endpoints running vulnerable N-central agent software. Threat telemetry tracks automated scanning arrays actively attempting to exploit exposed agent listening ports to execute arbitrary commands with SYSTEM privileges and deploy ransomware payloads across managed client networks. N-able issued server and agent patch updates, mandating immediate upgrades to N-central release 2026.3 or later.

Subverting central RMM software agents introduces severe systemic supply-chain risks across managed service providers and enterprise IT departments. Because N-central agents hold elevated SYSTEM rights to execute maintenance scripts, push software updates, and manage endpoints, an unauthenticated agent compromise allows threat actors to gain total host control and deploy malicious software fleet-wide.

- Upgrade central N-able N-central server installations to build version 2026.3 and push automatic agent updates across all endpoints.
- Block external public internet access to N-central agent communication ports, enforcing agent-to-server connections through encrypted tunnels.
- Inspect endpoint process logs and event logs for unverified PowerShell executions or script launches originating from agent services.
- Rotate agent authentication tokens and enforce mutual TLS mTLS verification across all agent-to-server communication channels.

RMM endpoint security requires immediate agent package updating and network port access restrictions to ensure remote management infrastructure remains protected against unauthenticated command execution exploits. #CodeDefence #Nable #Ncentral #RMM #SupplyChain #ZeroDay #RCE #Deserialization #EndpointSecurity #AppSec #PatchManagement #CISA
  ]]></description>
